    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d733">

                <pb facs="0763" n="505" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg505"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1054</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 363]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-08-10">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 10, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received your despatch
                    No. 394 of April 14, 1915,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d733-f1"/> in regard to possible
                    plans for rendering the shipping from America to neutral countries less
                    hazardous than it is at present.</p>
                <p>The Department does not perceive any objection to your suggesting a meeting of
                    shipping interests, provided, of course, you act entirely unofficially in
                    bringing it about. As to subsequent participation or recognition by this
                    Government, even unofficially, the proposals or plans resulting from the meeting
                    would have to be considered. In case you undertake to set on foot such a
                    meeting, the <pb facs="0764" n="506" xml:id="pg506"/> Department desires that
                    you make it clear that you act merely in an advisory capacity and in no respect
                    officially.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilbur J. Carr</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
